ence is our attitude and we've  often talked about motivation of course  you have to be more  but I think another big thing is what I  call the willingness to accept  uncertainty I am quite happy listening  to something that I don't understand and  listen to it over and over again and and  always having the same parts of it that  I don't understand now I like to listen  and then read so that and then look up  the words I have a chance to understand  it when I go back to listen again I  still don't understand certain parts  certain parts just take longer to learn  but I have always preferred to read and  to listen to things that were authentic  real language even if I didn't  understand but I watch all kinds of  learners who have a totally different  approach they want slow language they  want it to be everything has to be  explained they want to nail down the  pronunciation right at the beginning you  know I was listening to this ESL podcast  which is very popular far more popular  than we are and they do a great job but  I would never listen to that if I were  learning it's the equivalent podcast  we're available in Russian or Portuguese  or one of the languages that I'm  learning I don't want to hear people  saying well that is a nice building I  wonder how they built the building I  don't want that and so I very quickly in  my Russian studies went to audiobooks  went to Eric I mostly went to places  where I was struggling I can remember in  my Chinese studies I had this one  audiobook version a history of the  Second World War I must have listened to  that about 15 times not to mention my  sunshine and especially where there's  rhythm in the way things are said I mean  and I don't understand it but I think  that when we learn languages we have to  connect with the language at a level of  the tsardom that the music the rhythm  the feel of the language that's what we  have to establish and trying to nail  down little bits of grammar or nail down  how something is pronounced the L and  the R if you're Japanese or tall  if you're trying to learn Chinese or you  know the Spanish under our you know  relief whatever these things will come  and you'll have an easier time of it  later on in the meantime just let it go  learn to live with uncertainty vagueness  imperfection and learn to enjoy it and I  think that all successful language  learners have that in common that they  don't mind they don't mind the  imperfection and the people who try to  pursue imperfection and get frustrated  because they can't do it they can't  remember and they can't pronounce and  they get all kinds of negatives built up  those are the unsuccessful language  learners so a short word today I was  just thinking of it as I was how I got  to ESL podcast it was medicine somewhere  I went there to listen to it and a  tremendously successful and I am sure  that the vast majority of English  learners far prefer ESL podcast to  anything that we do yet when I listen to  you know natural podcasts and of course  all the offer is the podcast natural  language and then say come to link and  look up the words you need like again I  don't want lots of explanations ESL  podcasts offers you grammar notes and  explanations and exercises and  vocabulary I don't want to do that I'll  find my own vocabulary and I'll just  listen to it and gradually absorb it I'm  the exception  most people don't agree with me but on  the other hand I am better than the  average in language train so maybe maybe  maybe it's not just me anyway I look  forward to your comments bye for now
 

